      "id": "a1_u03_l01",
      "level": "A1",
      "unit": 3,
      "title": "De zéro à vingt",
      "description": "Master the numbers from zero to twenty.",
      "estimatedMinutes": 12,
      "sections": [
        {
          "type": "learn",
          "title": "Learn",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "heading": "Zero to ten",
              "body": "Let's review and complete the first numbers: zéro (0), un (1), deux (2), trois (3), quatre (4), cinq (5), six (6), sept (7), huit (8), neuf (9), dix (10). You already met most of them when talking about age.",
              "examples": [
                { "french": "zéro, un, deux, trois, quatre, cinq", "english": "zero, one, two, three, four, five" },
                { "french": "six, sept, huit, neuf, dix", "english": "six, seven, eight, nine, ten" },
                { "french": "J'ai dix doigts.", "english": "I have ten fingers." },
                { "french": "Il y a sept jours dans une semaine.", "english": "There are seven days in a week." }
              ],
              "tip": "\"Six\" and \"dix\" end in an s sound on their own (seess, deess), but before a consonant the x goes silent: six livres = \"see leevr\"."
            },
            {
              "heading": "Eleven to twenty",
              "body": "Eleven to sixteen are single words: onze (11), douze (12), treize (13), quatorze (14), quinze (15), seize (16). Then French builds on ten: dix-sept (17), dix-huit (18), dix-neuf (19) — literally \"ten-seven, ten-eight, ten-nine\". Twenty is vingt.",
              "examples": [
                { "french": "onze, douze, treize", "english": "eleven, twelve, thirteen" },
                { "french": "quatorze, quinze, seize", "english": "fourteen, fifteen, sixteen" },
                { "french": "dix-sept, dix-huit, dix-neuf, vingt", "english": "seventeen, eighteen, nineteen, twenty" },
                { "french": "Ma sœur a quinze ans.", "english": "My sister is fifteen years old." },
                { "french": "J'habite au numéro douze.", "english": "I live at number twelve." }
              ],
              "tip": "The g and t of \"vingt\" are silent — it sounds like \"van\" with a nasal vowel."
            }
          ]
        },
