    {
      "id": "a1_u03_l02",
      "level": "A1",
      "unit": 3,
      "title": "De vingt à cent",
      "description": "Count by tens to one hundred — including the famous French seventies, eighties and nineties.",
      "estimatedMinutes": 14,
      "sections": [
        {
          "type": "learn",
          "title": "Learn",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "heading": "The tens",
              "body": "The tens up to sixty are easy: vingt (20), trente (30), quarante (40), cinquante (50), soixante (60). One hundred is \"cent\".",
              "examples": [
                { "french": "vingt, trente, quarante", "english": "twenty, thirty, forty" },
                { "french": "cinquante, soixante", "english": "fifty, sixty" },
                { "french": "cent", "english": "one hundred" },
                { "french": "Mon père a cinquante ans.", "english": "My father is fifty years old." },
                { "french": "Cent euros, c'est cher !", "english": "A hundred euros, that's expensive!" }
              ]
            },
            {
              "heading": "70, 80, 90 — French does math!",
              "body": "There is no single word for seventy, eighty or ninety. French counts: soixante-dix (70 = 60 + 10), quatre-vingts (80 = 4 × 20), quatre-vingt-dix (90 = 80 + 10). After 70 and 90, keep counting with the teens: soixante-quinze (75 = 60 + 15), quatre-vingt-quinze (95 = 80 + 15).",
              "examples": [
                { "french": "soixante-dix", "english": "seventy (60 + 10)" },
                { "french": "quatre-vingts", "english": "eighty (4 × 20)" },
                { "french": "quatre-vingt-dix", "english": "ninety (80 + 10)" },
                { "french": "quatre-vingt-quinze", "english": "ninety-five (80 + 15)" },
                { "french": "soixante-douze", "english": "seventy-two (60 + 12)" },
                { "french": "quatre-vingt-huit", "english": "eighty-eight (80 + 8)" }
              ],
              "tip": "In Belgium and Switzerland you'll hear \"septante\" (70) and \"nonante\" (90) — much simpler, but France sticks to the math!"
            }
          ]
        },
