        {
          "type": "grammar",
          "title": "Grammar",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "heading": "Building numbers",
              "body": "For 21, 31, 41, 51, 61 and 71, French adds \"et un\" (and one): vingt et un, trente et un... soixante et onze (71). All other combinations use a hyphen: vingt-deux (22), trente-cinq (35), quarante-huit (48). \"Quatre-vingts\" keeps its final s only when nothing follows: quatre-vingts (80) but quatre-vingt-un (81).",
              "examples": [
                { "french": "vingt et un", "english": "twenty-one", "note": "with \"et\"" },
                { "french": "vingt-deux", "english": "twenty-two", "note": "with a hyphen" },
                { "french": "soixante et onze", "english": "seventy-one (60 + 11)" },
                { "french": "quatre-vingt-un", "english": "eighty-one", "note": "no s on vingt" },
                { "french": "cinquante et un", "english": "fifty-one", "note": "with \"et\"" },
                { "french": "trente-cinq", "english": "thirty-five", "note": "with a hyphen" }
              ],
              "tip": "Say the math out loud while you learn: 75 = \"sixty-fifteen\", 92 = \"four-twenty-twelve\". It quickly becomes natural!"
            }
          ]
        },
